<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huahine</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huahine is an island in the Leeward group of the Society Islands in French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>, made up of two parts, Huahine Nui and Huahine Iti, joined by a narrow channel. Settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a> perhaps a thousand years ago, it has one of the largest concentrations of marae in the Pacific, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeva">Maeva</a>. It remained an independent kingdom until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed it in 1895.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huahine.jpg" alt="Huahine" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HuahineISS006-E-38879.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Huahine is an island among the Society Islands of French Polynesia, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a> group. It is about 16 kilometres long and 13 kilometres wide, with a land area of 75 square kilometres, and consists of two main islands, Huahine Nui to the north and Huahine Iti to the south, separated by a few hundred metres of water and joined by a sandspit at low tide and by a small bridge.</p><p>A fringing coral reef with several islets surrounds them. Its highest point is Turi, at 669 metres. In the north-west of Huahine Nui a brackish lake, Lac Fauna Nui, is all that remains of an ancient atoll lagoon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human presence on Huahine goes back at least a thousand years, as shown by the many marae, and archaeologists estimate that the Maʻohi people colonized it from at least the 9th century CE. It holds one of the largest concentrations of Polynesian remains dated between 850 and 1100.</p><p>Until the late 19th century Huahine was an independent kingdom, which tradition says passed through three dynasties: the Hau-moʻo-rere dynasty, founded in the 17th century, whose last representative was Queen Tehaʻapapa I; the Tamatoa dynasty, which reigned until 1854; and the Teururaʻi dynasty, which reigned from 1854 to 1895. Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> arrived at Fare Harbour on 16 July 1769 and met the chief Ori, and returned on 3 September 1773.</p><p>In 1846 the island resisted French rule and in 1847 proclaimed itself the Kingdom of Huahine. On 20 April 1879 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> signed a treaty of friendship and commerce with its government. French warships bombarded the two largest villages in September 1890. On 15 September 1895 the regent, for Queen Tehaʻapapa, and the principal chiefs renounced their powers in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the island was incorporated into the French Establishments of Oceania. Protestant missionaries first reached the island in 1809, and in 1815 the mission ordered the destruction of the idols of the old gods.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Huahine is a commune of French Polynesia; its administrative centre is Fare, on Huahine Nui, and it had 6,263 inhabitants at the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Most people are Christian. The main activities are vanilla cultivation, copra production, fishing and tourism. The island is one of four where the Hawaiki Nui Vaʻa canoe race is held, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bora-bora">Bora Bora</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahaa">Tahaa</a> and Raiatea. The Faʻahia archaeological site has revealed remains of extinct birds killed off by the earliest Polynesian settlers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Huahine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From March 1844 Tahitians resisted French occupation, fighting at Mahaena in April and holding mountain strongholds until the French stormed the fort of Fautaua in December 1846, while forces on <b>Huahine</b> defeated a French landing in 1846.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-tahiti">Kingdom of Tahiti · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Huahine</b>, two islands joined by a narrow channel in the Leeward group of the Society Islands, was settled by Polynesians perhaps a thousand years ago.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-huahine">Kingdom of Huahine</a></li></ul></section>
